# route-lab — agent runbook

For ANY agent (Claude/Fable, Sol/Codex, Opus, others). Everything here is
plain CLIs + files: no model-specific skills or MCPs required. Read
`HANDOVER.md` for the current experiment state; this file is the stable
how-to.

## Repo layout (umbrella since 2026-07-18)

This is the ONE repo for the DM3 bot lab (GitHub: `Xerialen/route-lab`,
private):

- `route_lab/` + `ops/` + `tests/` + `docs/` + `artifacts/` — the route
  tooling, acceptance runners, and evidence (this runbook's subject).
- `match-deck/` — the match/bench MCP server and web deck (imported from
  the former `Xerialen/bot-control-deck`, history preserved; its own
  `AGENTS.md`/`README.md` apply inside that directory). Untracked runtime
  data (demos/logs) lives there too and stays out of git.
- `qw-nav-viewer/` — WebGPU navmesh viewer + naveditor (imported
  2026-07-19 from an untracked dir; see its `README.md`). Serves on
  `:8088`; overlays speak `qw-nav-overlay/1` / `qw-nav-graph/1` /
  `qw-nav-patch/1`. NOTE: the old path
  `...\quakeworld\nanobots\qw-nav-viewer` is an NTFS junction here —
  same files, don't treat them as two copies. `target/`+`dist/` are
  ignored; `.gitattributes` pins bytes (`-text`) since builds run in WSL.
- The **store-dm3 corpus is data, not code**: it lives OUTSIDE the repo at
  `C:\Users\benya\projects\quakeworld\data\store-dm3` (WSL
  `/mnt/c/...`), env override `QW_DM3_CORPUS`. Upstream source: HF dataset
  `Xerial/qw-demos-mined-db`; see `docs/dm3-mined-corpus-handoff.md` for
  how the slice was pulled and verified. On a new machine: clone this
  repo, pull the corpus separately, `uv sync`, done.
- The canonical Rust bot source is NOT here either — see "Hard rules".

## Team roster — seats, deliverables, interaction contract

**Naming rule (owner 2026-07-20):** all planfile rows, orders, reports
and GH comments refer to seats by FUNCTION (Orchestrator, dm3-operator,
navmesh-worker, bot-code, Auditor) — never by model/instance name.
Model names may appear only when the model choice itself is the topic.
Seats are functions: any LLM must be able to take a seat from repo
documentation alone (hot-swap goal). The Orchestrator seat's operational
handbook is `docs/orchestration-runbook.md`.

The lab runs as a multi-agent team. **Seats are functions, not models** —
any capable agent can fill any seat (current occupant + herdr pane noted
only so you can find each other). Everything here is plain CLIs + files.
The live coordination surface is the active planfile under `docs/plans/`
(today: `2026-07-19-spawn7-orchestration.md`): **append-only, sign every
row with your seat name + `[HH:MM]`, never edit another seat's rows.**

### Seat: Orchestrator / Reviewer (VPS session since 2026-07-21; formerly pane `w1:pQ`)

- **Expertise:** architecture, QW movement physics, cross-lane planning,
  code review, git.
- **Owns:** task decomposition, all reviews, ALL commits in the bot-code
  worktree, build/deploy orchestration, independent re-verification of
  every worker claim (reruns tests, rechecks data, browser-checks visuals).
- **Delivers:** review verdicts + commits → Bot-code worker; orders with
  explicit acceptance criteria → all seats; status with evidence → owner.
- **Never:** writes bot code itself (owner directive 2026-07-19), starts a
  heavy build without announcing it in the planfile first.

#### Orchestrator sub-agents (spawned by the Orchestrator; owner-ordered 2026-07-21)

The Orchestrator seat was serialising on mechanical work — sweep data
gathering, handoff intake, review preparation — while decisions queued
behind it. These sub-agents parallelise the mechanics. Every decision,
verdict, order, commit and planfile row stays with the Orchestrator.
Shared rules (all five, same spirit as drill-runner):

- **Read-only against lab state:** never a planfile append, commit, push,
  pane keystroke, GH post, deploy, cfg edit or server command. The only
  mutation allowed is a contained test/build run (review-prep), under the
  standard lock rules (`heavy.sh`/`remote_heavy.sh`; a held lock = WAIT).
- Output goes to the Orchestrator only; sub-agents never write the
  planfile and never message other seats.
- Every claim in a sub-agent report carries raw evidence (command output,
  file:line, artifact rows) — the Orchestrator re-verifies before acting.

The five (definitions live with the Orchestrator's session,
`.claude/agents/*.md` on its host; roles are the contract here):

- **sweep-scout** — one sweep's worth of status gathering in one spawn:
  hub server list (BIG port drift), bench-series status, herdr pane
  reads (read-only, no keystrokes), planfile tail, PR/issue counters,
  rtx_watch check. Returns a structured digest; the Orchestrator
  decides and acts.
- **handoff-verifier** — fetches a named owner handoff (gdrive
  `dumpen/bots/`), decodes it, and verifies every operative claim
  against repo/live state before any plan exists ("never take the
  prompt at its word"). Returns a claim-by-claim table with evidence
  and deviations flagged. No planning, no orders.
- **review-prep** — given a worktree + `base..tip`: produces the diff
  walkthrough, checks hard-rule compliance (forbidden cvars, formatter
  touch, map-specific hacks, corpus data in code, red-first evidence
  present), and runs/babysits the independent test rerun under
  containment. Returns an evidence package; the review VERDICT and the
  commit remain the Orchestrator's.
- **artifact-analyst** — forensics over `artifacts/*.jsonl` and
  telemetry: rates, windows, recidivism classes, sample slicing.
  Claims from filtered data are re-checked against raw rows before
  delivery (interaction-contract rule).
- **scribe** — drafting only, from facts supplied in the spawn order:
  GH round comments (must open with the attribution prefix), STATE.md
  updates, planfile row texts. The Orchestrator reviews, then
  posts/commits/appends personally.

