## Search campaigns (jump/movement certification) — mandatory method

Distilled from the 2026-07-18 method eval
(`docs/plans/2026-07-18-curl-search-method-eval.md`); applies to EVERY new
bounded-lattice movement search, any agent:

1. **Survey before searching.** Step 0 is always: hull-traced floor-height
   grid over the search area + cell inventory + a per-tick trace mode in
   the harness. Never run a lattice round against unmapped geometry
   (13 of 15 rounds in the gap-1 campaign were guesses the floor map
   answered instantly).
2. **Turn-budget arithmetic before the campaign.** Compare the entry
   envelope's heading with the corpus takeoff heading; QW air-accel gives
   only ~3-4 deg/tick at ~470 u/s. If the budget doesn't close, the
   contract is closed analytically - don't burn profiles proving it.
3. **Closure claims cover exactly the searched contract.** Analytical
   extrapolations are marked CONJECTURE and probe-checked cheaply before
   they steer the next campaign.
4. **Edge-hit check:** if the winning profile sits on the lattice edge in
   any dimension, widen that dimension and re-run before trusting the
   certificate.
5. Keep: fail-closed seed provenance, wall-bin histograms, negative-result
   docs with SHA-pinned artifacts.

## vmonster is the DEFAULT for heavy search/navmesh campaigns (owner directive 2026-07-19)

Any batch-scale run that builds navmeshes or sweeps a search lattice
(certification campaigns, gt-search shards, recertification of candidate
sets, future route searches) runs on **vmonster** (bisapps001, 64 threads,
1 TB RAM), NOT on pinnacle. Standard tool:

```bash
bash ops/vmonster_search_run.sh ship-run <job> <locally-built-bin> <inputs...> -- <args-template>
bash ops/vmonster_search_run.sh status  <job-stamp>
bash ops/vmonster_search_run.sh fetch   <job-stamp> <destdir>
```

Rules that make this safe/correct:

- **Build locally in WSL, ship the binary.** WSL Ubuntu 24.04 == vmonster;
  pinned codegen (no `-C target-cpu=native`, default f32) gives bit parity.
  No toolchain is installed on vmonster.
- **vmonster proposes, pinnacle verifies.** Everything fetched home passes
  the local fail-closed recertification gate before anything is armed.
  Never runtime/live loads on vmonster.
- **Containment is built in** (tmux + `timeout` + `ulimit -v` + wave cap):
  it is a corporate box — no sudo, our directories only, nothing that
  disturbs company infra.
- **Transfers need pacbookmo awake** (two-hop ProxyJump; direct SSH times
  out). The run itself survives in tmux once started.
- **What stays local:** the dev loop (cargo test, single probes, one-off
  navmesh builds ~minutes) and anything needed when pacbookmo is asleep.
  Local heavy runs still follow the containment section below.

## LANISTER build node — `ops/remote_heavy.sh` (cargo builds/tests off pinnacle)

For cargo **builds and test suites** (not search/navmesh campaigns — those go
to vmonster) that must not contend with pinnacle's live servers or the owner's
desktop, offload them to LANISTER's WSL over the tailnet. Seats and canonical
state stay on pinnacle; LANISTER only compiles/tests. Toolchain is pinned to
match pinnacle exactly (rustc/cargo 1.96.0). Run it from pinnacle WSL:

```bash
# a test suite (result must match a local run bit-for-bit):
ops/remote_heavy.sh --worktree /mnt/c/Users/benya/projects/quakeworld/ra-on-main \
    -- cargo test --release -p rtx-game --lib -- --test-threads=8

# a build, fetching one artifact home:
ops/remote_heavy.sh --worktree /mnt/c/Users/benya/projects/quakeworld/ra-on-main \
    --artifact target/release/librtx.so -- cargo build --release -p rtx-game
```

Rules that make it safe/correct:

- **Capture precedence, fail-closed.** Before every job it ssh-checks the
  node's Windows `tasklist` and REFUSES if a capture/game process is running
  (`obs64.exe`, `ezquake*`, `quakeworld*` — the list is a variable at the top
  of the script). If the check itself cannot run, it refuses — never builds
  blind (exit 3). The node doubles as the demoshots/capture host, so a build
  must never land mid-capture.
- **The node's three measurement servers are off-limits.** This script only
  compiles/tests and never speaks to a server port; do not point any build at,
  or otherwise touch, the running mvdsv slots on the node.
- **Node-wide build lock.** Builds run under `flock -n` on
  `~/route-lab-build/.build.lock`; a held lock means WAIT, not queue (same
  rule as `ops/heavy.sh`). Holder info (cmd/pid/host/src) is written into the
  lock file.
- **Uncommitted edits included.** It rsyncs the worktree (`-az --delete`,
  excluding `target/` and `.git` — the worktree `.git` is only a pointer file)
  to `~/route-lab-build/src/<worktree-basename>/`. The per-worktree remote
  `target/` cache is preserved across runs (excluded from `--delete`), so
  repeat builds are incremental (~1 s warm vs ~25 s cold for rtx-game).
- **Fixture is persistent.** `RTX_TEST_BSP=~/route-lab-build/fixtures/dm3.bsp`
  is exported for the build; the bsp lives outside `/tmp` so it survives a WSL
  restart (sha `aec9edbb…`, same as pinnacle's heavy.sh default).
- **Output + exit code passthrough.** Full stdout/stderr streams back; exit
  code == the remote command's. With `--artifact`, the file is fetched to
  `<worktree>/target/remote/<basename>` (never overwriting local
  `target/release/`) and its sha256 is printed and cross-checked.
- Tailnet only: the node is `benya@lanister.tail01b632.ts.net` (a variable in
  the script head); never a `192.168.*` address.

## Resource containment (mandatory for heavy runs)

The lab shares pinnaclewin11 with the owner's interactive desktop (Chrome,
Obsidian, herdr). WSL gets 12 of 16 cores; an uncontained parallel run
saturates them at normal priority and the Windows UI stutters (incident
2026-07-18: `cargo test -p rtx-nav` at ~14 cores froze the desktop).
Therefore, for every CPU-heavy invocation (test suites, builds, batch
sims, cohort builds):

- Prefix with `nice -n 19`: `nice -n 19 cargo test --locked -p rtx-nav ...`
- Cap test parallelism: add `-- --test-threads=8` to `cargo test`.
- Compute-heavy suites (rtx-nav, cohort builds) run `--release` unless a
  debug build is specifically needed — debug is 10-30x slower for this
  workload.
- mvdsv harness servers running faster-than-realtime sims are expected to
  burn CPU; still start them under `nice -n 19` when scripted.

This does not apply to short commands (CLIs, single tests, deploys).

## Hard rules

- Human corpus data is observation/calibration evidence ONLY - never
  trajectories/usercmds into bot code (see `docs/dm3-qwd-route-search.md`).
- Never modify files under `artifacts/` - they are write-once evidence.
- The bot-code lane's canonical Rust worktree is `../curl-c-fix`
  (branch curl-c-fix, origin Xerialen/4on4) — see the checklist pointer.
  `../rex-fix-netclient-combat` is LEGACY/frozen (earlier lane); never
  reset/format/broad-stage either worktree.
- Acceptance runs: fresh JSONL artifact name each time, control port 27954
  only, see `HANDOVER.md`.
- CPU-heavy runs are always contained (`nice -n 19`, `--test-threads=8`,
  `--release`) — see "Resource containment" above.
- On every rtx upstream sync: diff `cvars.rs` defaults FIRST. The
  `rtx_walljump` default-ON incident (2026-07-19) invalidated a full day
  of measurement data.
- Planted links die on every navmesh rebuild — drivers replant after map
  restart. Patch application must run in trial gaps (the global trial
  guard blocks planlink/cell mid-trial).
- Never run a formatter in write mode across the tree; match local style
  by hand (bot-code incident 2026-07-19: `cargo fmt --all` touched 96
  undeclared files).


