# QW lab live viewer — streaming game telemetry to a browser

Playbook for putting a **live view of a running game-server experiment in a browser**: tail the
server's log, push per-tick JSON over a websocket, render a three.js map+trajectory panel beside
an in-engine QTV stream. Harvested from the komodobots/local-hub `/botlab` build.

last harvested: komodobots@e9f131c + local-hub@bb3cb38 (2026-06-09)

## Recipes

- **Stdlib-only websocket push server** (no pip on the target host): hand-rolled RFC6455 is ~80
  lines for the server→client direction — HTTP 101 handshake (`Sec-WebSocket-Accept` =
  b64(sha1(key+GUID))), unmasked text frames out, consume client frames only to answer ping
  (0x9→0xA) and close (0x8). asyncio `start_server` + a poll-tail loop is plenty for ~100 Hz ×
  ~200 B frames. Evidence: `komodobots scripts/telemetry_ws.py` (79cf5a7); live run
  `20260609T182853Z` streamed 3825 frames to a browser with zero loss.
- **Quake BSP → three.js map mesh**: `demopasha/phase0/bsp_parse.py <map.bsp> <outdir>` emits a
  Wavefront OBJ (needs numpy+construct — throwaway venv is fine). Render with two
  `MeshBasicMaterial`s (translucent fill + wireframe child — the OBJ has no normals, so unlit
  basic material is the right call), and rotate the whole object `rotation.x = -π/2` for Quake
  Z-up → three Y-up; per-point mapping is `(x,y,z)q → (x,z,-y)three`. dm3 = 9 256 tris / 261 KB —
  loads instantly. Evidence: local-hub a7b48dd (`deploy/botlab-assets/dm3.obj`,
  `src/pages/botlab/quakeCoords.ts`).
- **Factor a parser out of a script with a parity proof**: move the regex + field-mapping
  *verbatim* into a module, re-parse a real artifact, and assert the output equals the old code's
  committed JSON (`old == new` on the full structure, not spot checks). Evidence: komodobots
  79cf5a7 — 3845/3845 command rows + replay/qwd events byte-identical on run `20260609T172810Z`.
- **Validate a live stream against post-run ground truth, exactly**: attach a recorder websocket
  in the page (`evaluate_script`, accumulate `{count, max_vh, min_dist}` per run_id), run the
  attempt, then compare against the post-run summary artifact. Demand **equality**, not
  plausibility — both the replay (3845 / 473.6 / 8.4) and the live run (3825 / 478.4 / 307.9)
  matched `trace_summary.json` exactly because live and post-run share one parser.
- **Replay rig for UI work without the live lab**: feed a real captured `screen.log` into a fake
  runs dir in timed chunks (split mid-line to exercise partial-line tailing) and point the sidecar
  at it. Evidence: `$TEMP/botlab-test/feed_replay.py` pattern, used for all pre-live iterations.

## Pitfalls

- **`Object3D.traverse` + adding children during the walk → `Maximum call stack size exceeded`.**
  `traverse()` iterates children live, so a wireframe mesh added as a child gets visited, gets its
  own child, recurses forever. Fix: collect targets into an array during traverse, mutate after.
  Why: three.js does not snapshot the children list. Evidence: local-hub bb3cb38.
- **"Newest run dir" by lexical name breaks on mixed naming.** Hand-named dirs
  (`solo_realip_d180`) sort after timestamp ids (`20260609T…`), so max-by-name latched onto a
  stale run while the real attempt was live. Fix: `max(key=st_mtime)`. Why: name order == time
  order only holds for a uniform naming scheme. Evidence: komodobots e9f131c, caught live.
- **A sidecar restart mid-attempt silently drops the backlog for late joiners.** The tailer reads
  the whole existing log in its first poll; clients that connect 1–2 s later miss those frames
  (broadcast only reaches currently-connected sockets). Fix for tooling: attach listeners *before*
  starting the run; design fix (v2) would be a per-client replay-from-start.
- **ufw default-deny eats new LAN websockets.** servexeri allows only enumerated ports; a new
  sidecar port (8770) is unreachable from LAN until a rule is added. `ssh -L` is the zero-config
  workaround for verification; the rule itself is an ask-first change.

## Patterns

- **Live side-by-side viewer**: per-tick telemetry websocket (3D panel + HUD) beside the engine's
  own QTV stream pulled through a loopback-locked relay — the QTV is buffered slightly behind the
  raw telemetry; "both live, eyeball-synced" is the v1 contract. `new_attempt` control frames
  (run_id, port, map from `run.env`) let one page session follow consecutive ephemeral servers
  without reload: clear trail, re-issue `qtvplay`, reset HUD accumulators.
- **HUD over a 100 Hz stream**: subscribe with raw callbacks, accumulate derived state in refs
  (hops = onground 1→0 transitions, air time, yaw rate), push React state at ~12 Hz. Never
  setState per frame.

## Known limits

- FTE's `enableLogToEvents` console shim (`fte/log.ts`) calls `message.startsWith` on every
  `console.log` arg — non-string logs from any library on the same page throw. Pre-existing
  upstream behavior; keep page logging to strings or fix the shim.
- `ws://` telemetry from an `https://` page is mixed content — v1 is LAN/dev http only; off-LAN
  needs a `wss://` tunnel route (deliberately parked).

## Sources

- `Xerialen/komodobots` PR #59 (`botlab-telemetry`), `Xerialen/local-hub` PR #1
  (`feat/botlab-viewer`, code as `deploy/frontend-botlab.patch`)
- Plan: `~/.claude/plans/encapsulated-forging-kite.md`
- Related: [[quake-servers]], [[demopasha]] BSP tooling, local-hub `docs/current-stage.md`


===== C:\Users\benya\Workspace\thevault\claude\browser-testing-setup.md =====
# browser-testing-setup

The two-MCP browser testing rig on [[pinnacle-win11]]. Set up 2026-05-24.

The policy this implements is in `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` STEP 2.5 ("Validate before reporting" → "Web app / UI change"): **chrome-devtools MCP for debug introspection, Playwright MCP for flow validation, both attached to the same Chrome instance** so they share view of the page.

## Architecture

```
Win11 host                                       │  WSL2 (Ubuntu-24.04)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────
                                                 │
  Start Menu: "Chrome (debug 9222)"              │
       ↓ launches ~/bin/chrome-debug.ps1         │
       ↓                                         │
  chrome.exe                                     │
    --user-data-dir=~/.config/chrome-debug       │
    --remote-debugging-port=9222                 │
    --remote-debugging-address=127.0.0.1         │
       ↓                                         │
  CDP socket on 127.0.0.1:9222 ◄─────────────────┤── 127.0.0.1:9222 reachable from
                ▲                                │   WSL because .wslconfig has
                │                                │   networkingMode=mirrored
                │                                │
       chrome-devtools MCP                       │   playwright MCP
       (Win-side Claude Code)                    │   (WSL-side Claude Code)
       attaches via                              │   attaches via
       --browserUrl http://127.0.0.1:9222        │   --cdp-endpoint http://127.0.0.1:9222
```

Both MCPs talk to the **same** Chrome process and see the same DOM / network / console. They don't fight; CDP supports multiple clients per browser. Use whichever fits the task per CLAUDE.md:

- **chrome-devtools MCP** → debug-flavored (perf traces, network inspection, console errors, throttling, Core Web Vitals).
- **Playwright MCP** → flow-flavored (multi-step user journeys, form submission, snapshot+screenshot sequences, headless-style repeatable runs).

## Components

| Piece | Location | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Chrome launcher | `C:\Users\benya\bin\chrome-debug.ps1` | Idempotent — refuses to start a second copy on :9222 |
| Debug profile dir | `C:\Users\benya\.config\chrome-debug\` | Dedicated; do not point at daily Chrome profile |
| Shortcut installer | `C:\Users\benya\bin\_install-chrome-debug-shortcut.ps1` | One-shot — re-run if Start Menu shortcut is lost |
| Start Menu entry | `%APPDATA%\Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\Chrome (debug 9222).lnk` | Pinnable to taskbar |
| chrome-devtools MCP (Win) | `C:\Users\benya\.claude.json` → `mcpServers.chrome-devtools` | Already wired before this setup; `npx chrome-devtools-mcp@latest --browserUrl http://127.0.0.1:9222` |
| chrome-devtools MCP (WSL) | `~/.claude.json` → `mcpServers.chrome-devtools` | **Added 2026-06-29** via `claude mcp add chrome-devtools -s user -- npx chrome-devtools-mcp@latest --browserUrl http://127.0.0.1:9222`. So the WSL Claude Code session now has **both** `chrome-devtools` + `playwright` against the same `:9222` Chrome. Reaches Win's `:9222` via mirrored networking (same as Playwright). Was added to drive the `qwicker` expense flow on pinnacle. (Supersedes the old note below that chrome-devtools is Win-only — that reasoning predated this.) |
| Playwright MCP (WSL) | `~/.npm-global/bin/playwright-mcp` (npm `@playwright/mcp`) | Global install, user `xerial` |
| Playwright MCP registration | `~/.claude.json` in WSL via `claude mcp add playwright -s user --` | `... -- playwright-mcp --cdp-endpoint http://127.0.0.1:9222` |
| Networking mode | `C:\Users\benya\.wslconfig` → `[wsl2] networkingMode=mirrored` | Required: makes WSL's `localhost:9222` reach Win's `localhost:9222`. Takes effect on `wsl --shutdown`. |

## Daily usage

1. Click Start → "Chrome (debug 9222)" (or pin to taskbar). The launcher checks port 9222 isn't already taken and starts Chrome with the dedicated profile.
2. Open your app (`localhost:3000`, deployed URL, whatever).
3. In Claude Code on the Win side: use `mcp__chrome-devtools__*` tools — they attach to the live tab.
4. In Claude Code on the WSL side: use `mcp__playwright__*` tools — they attach to the same live tab via CDP.
5. When done, close the Chrome window. The profile persists at `~/.config/chrome-debug/` — your test-site logins survive between sessions.

## Gotcha — "I can't see the browser" (the attached :9222 Chrome may be windowless)

Playwright/chrome-devtools attach to *whatever* is listening on `:9222` — and that Chrome can be **headless or windowless** (a leftover from a prior automated run), so the agent drives it fine while the user sees nothing. The box accumulates stray CDP Chromes: profiles like `C:\Temp\komodo-cdp` (on :9222) and piles of headless `…\Temp\qwcdp-*` / `qwbom-cdp-*` (on :9444/:9445) from qwicker/qwbom/komodo runs. **Before any user-interactive CDP flow (login, SSO, "watch this"), confirm the driven Chrome has a real window:**

```powershell
# visible windows + titles — the :9222 tab's title should appear here:
Get-Process chrome | ? { $_.MainWindowTitle } | Select Id,MainWindowTitle
# is :9222 headless / which profile?
Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter "Name='chrome.exe'" |
  ? { $_.CommandLine -match 'remote-debugging-port=9222' } | % { $_.CommandLine }
```

If no MainWindowTitle matches the driven tab, the user genuinely can't see it — relaunch a **visible** debug-Chrome on :9222 (Start Menu "Chrome (debug 9222)") and re-attach. Don't assume "attached == visible". (Hit 2026-06-29 setting up the `qwicker` flow — burned several turns.)

**And the converse trap:** `Start-Process chrome --new-window <url>` does **not** give you a driveable browser — if normal Chrome is already running it just adds a window to that **default profile** (cmdline is bare `chrome.exe`, no `--remote-debugging-port`), so it's *visible but uncontrollable*, and Chrome won't let you enable a debug port on an already-running profile. **Visible AND driveable requires launching a fresh instance** with its own `--user-data-dir=<dedicated>` **and** `--remote-debugging-port=<port>` (non-headless). So "I opened a Chrome window" ≠ "I can drive it".

**Recipe — get a visible+driveable debug-Chrome on :9222 (proven 2026-06-29):**
```powershell
# 1. kill any windowless/stale :9222 instance (main browser proc only; children follow)
Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter "Name='chrome.exe'" |
  ? { $_.CommandLine -match 'remote-debugging-port=9222' -and $_.CommandLine -notmatch '--type=' } |
  % { Stop-Process -Id $_.ProcessId -Force }
# 2. launch a VISIBLE one with the dedicated profile
Start-Process 'C:\Program Files\Google\Chrome\Application\chrome.exe' -ArgumentList `
  '--remote-debugging-port=9222','--remote-debugging-address=127.0.0.1',`
  '--user-data-dir=C:\Users\benya\.config\chrome-debug','--new-window','about:blank'
```
Then: Playwright MCP is still bound to the *dead* browser — the first `browser_*` call errors `Target page… has been closed`; a throwaway `browser_tabs(list)` reconnects it to the new :9222. **Don't pass the target URL on the Chrome command line** — if normal Chrome is running it leaks the URL into the *default* profile (a stray window you can't drive); instead launch `about:blank` and `browser_navigate` the tab via the driver. Caveat: a fresh `chrome-debug` profile is logged into nothing — the user re-does every site login (Kwick, Microsoft/Outlook) once; the profile then persists them.

## Verifying the rig

After `wsl --shutdown` to apply mirrored networking, and after launching the debug Chrome, this should work from **both** sides:

```powershell
# From Win PowerShell:
curl http://127.0.0.1:9222/json/version
```

```bash
# From WSL bash:
curl http://127.0.0.1:9222/json/version
```

Both return the same JSON with `"Browser"`, `"webSocketDebuggerUrl"`, etc. If the WSL one fails but the Win one works, mirrored networking isn't active — re-check `.wslconfig` and re-run `wsl --shutdown`.

## Why not just one MCP for both sides?

- Running chrome-devtools MCP from WSL would mean launching a *Linux* Chrome via WSLg, which adds a GUI layer the user can't see in the Windows-native browser they're actually testing.
- Running Playwright MCP from Windows is fine but redundant — and Playwright's strength shines when it's part of a Linux dev workflow (CI parity, headless repeatability). Keeping it WSL-side aligns with the [[pinnacle-win11]] hosting policy ("Linux-native tooling lives in WSL2").
- Sharing one Chrome via CDP avoids any "your test passes here but breaks there" cookie/cache divergence.

## What this is NOT

- Not for daily browsing. Debug Chrome should never have your real Gmail / GitHub / bank logged in.
- Not a way to test Safari/Firefox. Playwright MCP supports those via its own bundled binaries (`--browser webkit|firefox`) — but for the rig described here we always attach to the running Chrome via CDP.
- Not a replacement for proper unit/integration tests. This is "did the change actually do the thing in a real browser" validation, the last mile before claiming a UI fix is done.

## See also

- [[tools]]
- [[pinnacle-win11]]
- `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` STEP 2.5 — the parent policy
- chrome-devtools MCP: <https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp>
- Playwright MCP: <https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp>

