0487: ## Search campaigns (jump/movement certification) — mandatory method
0488: 
0489: Distilled from the 2026-07-18 method eval
0490: (`docs/plans/2026-07-18-curl-search-method-eval.md`); applies to EVERY new
0491: bounded-lattice movement search, any agent:
0492: 
0493: 1. **Survey before searching.** Step 0 is always: hull-traced floor-height
0494:    grid over the search area + cell inventory + a per-tick trace mode in
0495:    the harness. Never run a lattice round against unmapped geometry
0496:    (13 of 15 rounds in the gap-1 campaign were guesses the floor map
0497:    answered instantly).
0498: 2. **Turn-budget arithmetic before the campaign.** Compare the entry
0499:    envelope's heading with the corpus takeoff heading; QW air-accel gives
0500:    only ~3-4 deg/tick at ~470 u/s. If the budget doesn't close, the
0501:    contract is closed analytically - don't burn profiles proving it.
0502: 3. **Closure claims cover exactly the searched contract.** Analytical
0503:    extrapolations are marked CONJECTURE and probe-checked cheaply before
0504:    they steer the next campaign.
0505: 4. **Edge-hit check:** if the winning profile sits on the lattice edge in
0506:    any dimension, widen that dimension and re-run before trusting the
0507:    certificate.
0508: 5. Keep: fail-closed seed provenance, wall-bin histograms, negative-result
0509:    docs with SHA-pinned artifacts.
0510: 
0511: ## vmonster is the DEFAULT for heavy search/navmesh campaigns (owner directive 2026-07-19)
0512: 
0513: Any batch-scale run that builds navmeshes or sweeps a search lattice
0514: (certification campaigns, gt-search shards, recertification of candidate
0515: sets, future route searches) runs on **vmonster** (bisapps001, 64 threads,
0516: 1 TB RAM), NOT on pinnacle. Standard tool:
0517: 
0518: ```bash
0519: bash ops/vmonster_search_run.sh ship-run <job> <locally-built-bin> <inputs...> -- <args-template>
0520: bash ops/vmonster_search_run.sh status  <job-stamp>
0521: bash ops/vmonster_search_run.sh fetch   <job-stamp> <destdir>
0522: ```
0523: 
0524: Rules that make this safe/correct:
0525: 
0526: - **Build locally in WSL, ship the binary.** WSL Ubuntu 24.04 == vmonster;
0527:   pinned codegen (no `-C target-cpu=native`, default f32) gives bit parity.
0528:   No toolchain is installed on vmonster.
0529: - **vmonster proposes, pinnacle verifies.** Everything fetched home passes
0530:   the local fail-closed recertification gate before anything is armed.
0531:   Never runtime/live loads on vmonster.
0532: - **Containment is built in** (tmux + `timeout` + `ulimit -v` + wave cap):
0533:   it is a corporate box — no sudo, our directories only, nothing that
0534:   disturbs company infra.
0535: - **Transfers need pacbookmo awake** (two-hop ProxyJump; direct SSH times
0536:   out). The run itself survives in tmux once started.
0537: - **What stays local:** the dev loop (cargo test, single probes, one-off
0538:   navmesh builds ~minutes) and anything needed when pacbookmo is asleep.
0539:   Local heavy runs still follow the containment section below.
0540: 
0541: ## LANISTER build node — `ops/remote_heavy.sh` (cargo builds/tests off pinnacle)
0542: 
0543: For cargo **builds and test suites** (not search/navmesh campaigns — those go
0544: to vmonster) that must not contend with pinnacle's live servers or the owner's
0545: desktop, offload them to LANISTER's WSL over the tailnet. Seats and canonical
0546: state stay on pinnacle; LANISTER only compiles/tests. Toolchain is pinned to
0547: match pinnacle exactly (rustc/cargo 1.96.0). Run it from pinnacle WSL:
0548: 
0549: ```bash
0550: # a test suite (result must match a local run bit-for-bit):
0551: ops/remote_heavy.sh --worktree /mnt/c/Users/benya/projects/quakeworld/ra-on-main \
0552:     -- cargo test --release -p rtx-game --lib -- --test-threads=8
0553: 
0554: # a build, fetching one artifact home:
0555: ops/remote_heavy.sh --worktree /mnt/c/Users/benya/projects/quakeworld/ra-on-main \
0556:     --artifact target/release/librtx.so -- cargo build --release -p rtx-game
0557: ```
0558: 
0559: Rules that make it safe/correct:
0560: 
0561: - **Capture precedence, fail-closed.** Before every job it ssh-checks the
0562:   node's Windows `tasklist` and REFUSES if a capture/game process is running
0563:   (`obs64.exe`, `ezquake*`, `quakeworld*` — the list is a variable at the top
0564:   of the script). If the check itself cannot run, it refuses — never builds
0565:   blind (exit 3). The node doubles as the demoshots/capture host, so a build
0566:   must never land mid-capture.
0567: - **The node's three measurement servers are off-limits.** This script only
0568:   compiles/tests and never speaks to a server port; do not point any build at,
0569:   or otherwise touch, the running mvdsv slots on the node.
0570: - **Node-wide build lock.** Builds run under `flock -n` on
0571:   `~/route-lab-build/.build.lock`; a held lock means WAIT, not queue (same
0572:   rule as `ops/heavy.sh`). Holder info (cmd/pid/host/src) is written into the
0573:   lock file.
0574: - **Uncommitted edits included.** It rsyncs the worktree (`-az --delete`,
0575:   excluding `target/` and `.git` — the worktree `.git` is only a pointer file)
0576:   to `~/route-lab-build/src/<worktree-basename>/`. The per-worktree remote
0577:   `target/` cache is preserved across runs (excluded from `--delete`), so
0578:   repeat builds are incremental (~1 s warm vs ~25 s cold for rtx-game).
0579: - **Fixture is persistent.** `RTX_TEST_BSP=~/route-lab-build/fixtures/dm3.bsp`
0580:   is exported for the build; the bsp lives outside `/tmp` so it survives a WSL
0581:   restart (sha `aec9edbb…`, same as pinnacle's heavy.sh default).
0582: - **Output + exit code passthrough.** Full stdout/stderr streams back; exit
0583:   code == the remote command's. With `--artifact`, the file is fetched to
0584:   `<worktree>/target/remote/<basename>` (never overwriting local
0585:   `target/release/`) and its sha256 is printed and cross-checked.
0586: - Tailnet only: the node is `benya@lanister.tail01b632.ts.net` (a variable in
