===== RUNBOOK =====
# Movement Lab operator runbook

This is the user and operator manual for the QuakeWorld Movement Lab at
`qw-nav-viewer/`. It describes the functionality that is actually available in the current UI,
the data contracts it accepts, and the safe workflow for producing reviewable navmesh changes.

The short architectural overview remains in [README.md](README.md). This runbook is the canonical
answer to “how do I operate the lab?”

Last verified against the application and its browser flow: **2026-07-21**.

## Documentation contract

The runbook is part of the feature, not a post-hoc note:

- Any user-visible control, keyboard shortcut, URL parameter, data contract, service command, or
  patch workflow change must update this file in the same commit.
- Every functional control panel in `index.html` carries a `data-runbook` marker. The Rust test
  `runbook_covers_every_control_panel` fails if a panel lacks a matching section here.
- Contract changes must update the relevant fixture in `overlays/`, parser tests, and the contract
  summary below.
- Browser-visible changes require a real-browser check. Put transient Playwright evidence under
  `output/playwright/`; put review evidence that must be committed under `validation/`.

## What the Movement Lab is

The lab is a read-only BSP/navmesh viewer plus two local authoring surfaces:

1. named visual annotations, which never alter navigation data; and
2. nav patch authoring, which produces JSON but never applies it to a server.

The viewer renders a bundled Quake BSP, a locally carved diagnostic graph, Nano's exported graph,
producer overlays, route diagnostics, near-field clearance, and patch edits in one WebGPU view.

The viewer does **not** control a bot, mutate `qwprogs`, send control verbs, deploy a patch, or write
to a game server. A downloaded patch becomes live only if a separate operator deliberately places
it in a driver-polled location.

## Start, access, and stop

The normal installation is the user service:

```bash
systemctl --user status qw-nav-viewer.service
systemctl --user start qw-nav-viewer.service
systemctl --user restart qw-nav-viewer.service
journalctl --user -u qw-nav-viewer.service -f
```

The service runs `scripts/serve-dev.sh`, which starts:

- Trunk on `0.0.0.0:8088`; and
- a private no-cache overlay source server on `127.0.0.1:18089`.

Open one of:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8088
http://<movement-lab-host-LAN-IP>:8088
```

Use the LAN address from another computer or phone. The server should remain active after a normal
viewer task. Stop it only when explicitly retiring or repairing the service:

```bash
systemctl --user stop qw-nav-viewer.service
```

For an isolated foreground development run, first make sure the service is stopped or using another
port, then run:

```bash
cd qw-nav-viewer
bash scripts/serve-dev.sh
```

The canonical directory is `route-lab/qw-nav-viewer`. The old
`nanobots/qw-nav-viewer` path is an NTFS junction to the same directory, not another checkout.

## Five-minute orientation

1. Open the page and wait for the bottom status bar to turn ready.
2. Select a map in the header. `dm3.bsp` is the default.
3. Load a Nano graph under **Navigation graph** if the work concerns real bot navigation.
4. Use **Annotations** for review notes that must not affect the graph.
5. Use **Nav editor** only when authoring an explicit `qw-nav-patch/1` candidate.
6. Use **Ghost bot** and **Route goal** to inspect the viewer's local diagnostic A*.
7. Use the companion objective matrix to establish corpus timing baselines before live drills.
8. Export data to a download, collect browser evidence, and follow the reviewed patch workflow
   before anything reaches a DEV-polled path.

## Corpus objective baselines

The viewer does not query human demos, but the Movement Lab includes a companion aggregate-only
CLI in the route-lab root. Use it before a live objective drill to answer “how fast is this item
pair normally traversed?” without reading identities, paths, trajectories, or input commands:

```bash
cd ..
uv run dm3-objective-matrix build
uv run dm3-objective-matrix list --no-combat --sort samples --min-samples 100 --limit 25
uv run dm3-objective-matrix route ring quad --no-combat
```

The matrix requires that the target was available at the source pickup, that the same player made
the target's next global pickup, and that the runner did not respawn between them. `--no-combat`
means obituary-free, not damage-free. Results are timing distributions only and must not be read as
proof of a specific route or movement technique. Full reproduction details and the objective
registry are in [DM3 objective timing matrix](../docs/dm3-objective-matrix.md).

## Codex bot operator

The repo-packaged `qw-bot-control` plugin is the companion control surface for
the read-only viewer. It contributes the `$operate-qw-bot-lab` workflow and an
`rjtune` MCP server for an isolated RTX harness. Install it from the route-lab
root, then start a new Codex session:

```bash
codex plugin marketplace add "$PWD"
codex plugin add qw-bot-control@route-lab
```

The workflow requires an aggregate corpus baseline, owns only servers it
starts, and treats home-hub/QTV browser evidence as part of every live trial.
It does not turn the nav viewer into a control client. In particular, never
attach it to an already active shared control port: the game channel has one
outbound event writer. Detailed movement gates and current gaps (direct bot
shooting and a dedicated swim trial) live in the plugin skill references.

## Maps and camera

The bundled maps are:

- `dm3.bsp` — default;
- `dm2.bsp`;
- `e1m2.bsp`;
- `schloss.bsp`; and
- `phantombase.bsp`.

Selecting an overlay or graph for another bundled map switches the BSP automatically. Map changes
also load that map's browser-local annotation document.

Camera controls:

| Control | Effect |
|---|---|
| `W`, `A`, `S`, `D` | Fly horizontally relative to the camera |
| `Space` / `C` | Fly up / down |
| `Shift` | Fast fly while held |
| Hold right mouse | Mouse-look and lock the pointer |
| `Esc` | Release mouse-look |
| `F` or **Frame map** | Frame the complete BSP |
| **Frame data overlay** | Frame every marker, route point, and polyline in the loaded overlay |
| **Frame Nano graph** | Frame the currently loaded exported graph |
| `G` | Put the ghost at the camera and snap it to a local standable cell |
| `P` | Put the route goal at the camera and snap it to a local standable cell |

<!-- runbook:data-overlays -->
## Data overlays

The **Data overlay** panel loads producer-owned `qw-nav-overlay/1` documents. Built-in selector
entries include `overlay-demo`, `dm3-spawns`, and `dm3p8-bowl-crossings`; any safe filename can
also be opened with:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8088/?overlay=<name>
```

The viewer requests `overlays/<name>.json`. Names accept ASCII letters, digits, `_`, and `-` only.
The query string is updated when the selector changes, so a configured view can be bookmarked.

Overlay sections:

- `markers`: labelled 3D points. Recognized visual kinds are `spawn`, `item`, `rj-start`, and
  `rj-goal`; unknown kinds remain forward-compatible.
- `routes`: a route with `points` draws those producer points as authoritative truth. A route
  without `points` asks the local diagnostic graph for an A* path from `start` to `goal`.
- `polylines`: colored static trails with points shaped `[t_ms, x, y, z]`. V1 retains `t_ms` in
  data but has no time scrubber.

The status line reports marker count, authoritative routes, successful local A* fallbacks,
polylines, and names of unroutable fallbacks. Unknown JSON fields are ignored.

**Frame data overlay** focuses the camera on the loaded overlay's markers, authoritative route
points (or fallback endpoints), and polylines. It is the quickest way to find a small diagnostic
overlay inside a full-map graph.

The Trunk proxy serves `/overlays/*` from the source directory through the no-cache sidecar. Editing
an overlay does not require a WASM rebuild and the next HTTP request should see the new bytes.

<!-- runbook:navigation-graph -->
## Navigation graph

The **Navigation graph** panel has two independent sources:

- **Local diagnostic carve**: a coarse 32-unit graph built by `nav-core` from the selected BSP.
- **Nano exported truth**: the real exported graph loaded from
  `overlays/<name>-graph.json` using `qw-nav-graph/1`.

Use the exported Nano graph for link and patch decisions. The local carve is a diagnostic
comparison and route-visualization aid; it is not authoritative for live bot navigation.

Load a graph from the selector or URL:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8088/?graph=dm3
```

The selector also exposes `dm3p8`, the reviewed offline 8-unit sampling candidate. It is a
viewer-only artifact, not a deployed mesh. Its canonical graph image is
`9b5e7f5cadfa18212e454134da1538ed2330509192283de69b01b126394cf8fc` with 21,421 cells and
119,549 links. To inspect its 11 payload-certified bowl-corridor SpeedJumps without searching the
entire graph, use:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8088/?graph=dm3p8&overlay=dm3p8-bowl-crossings&kinds=SpeedJump
```

The `kinds=SpeedJump` filter hides unrelated graph-link kinds while keeping the p8 cells and all
SpeedJump links visible. Then click **Frame data overlay**. Cyan truth lines show all 11 payload
paths (8 north, 3 south); the three yellow labels show direction, profile count, and `v_req` range.
The expanded **Focused overlay routes** list names every link with direction, link index, and exact
`v_req`. The exact graph link
indices, takeoff points, `v_req`, airtime, chaining, curl gain, and `ground_turn` flag are retained
as forward-compatible `focus_links` metadata in the overlay JSON. This visualization is evidence
of emitted offline links only—it does not claim live execution success.

Loading an exported graph hides the local carve by default and enables Nano truth. Both toggles can
be enabled to compare them. Individual link kinds can be filtered:

`Walk`, `Step`, `Drop`, `JumpGap`, `DoubleJump`, `SpeedJump`, `Plat`, `Teleport`, `Hook`, and
`RocketJump`.

The graph contract stores:

```json
{
  "schema": "qw-nav-graph/1",
  "map": "dm3",
  "grid": 32.0,
  "cells": [[448.0, 192.0, 56.0]],
  "links": [[0, 1, "Walk", 0.1]]
}
```

Traversal-aware dumps may add `speed_jump_payloads`, `speed_jump_traversals`, and
`speed_jump_traversal_provenance`. Rendering ignores those sidecars, while audit tools use them to
bind a displayed link to its payload or `SpeedJumpTraversal`, ground-turn contract, launchbox,
yaw/envelope data, and rollout successor. The four-field `links` tuples remain stable.

<!-- runbook:annotations -->
## Named annotations

Annotations are review drawings. They are separate from overlays, graph links, and nav patches.

### Draw an arrow

1. Open **Annotations** in the left column.
2. Check **Draw mode**.
3. Select **Draw arrows** and a color.
4. Select a saved layer or enter a name and click **New layer**. If none exists, enabling Draw mode
   creates `Notes 1` for the first edit.
5. Hold the left mouse button on a visible navmesh surface, drag freely across the mesh, and release.

The endpoints are BSP/world coordinates, not screen pixels. Arrows stay attached to their map while
the camera moves. With a matching exported graph loaded, its nav cells are the drawing surface;
otherwise the local carve is used.

### Erase, name, and organize

- Select **Erase arrows**, then click the body of an arrow in the selected visible layer.
- Edit **Layer name** and click **Save / rename**.
- **Hide layer** removes it from the 3D view without deleting it; **Show layer** restores it.
- **Clear arrows** empties the selected layer.
- **Delete** removes the selected layer.
- The filled/empty circle in **Saved layers** indicates visible/hidden state.

Draw mode and Nav editor mode are mutually exclusive. Enabling one disarms the other.

### Persistence and transfer

Completed changes autosave per map in browser `localStorage` under
`qw-nav-viewer:annotations:<map>`. Storage is local to that browser profile and device. A phone and
desktop do not share annotations automatically.

**Download all layers** exports `<map>-annotations.json`. **Import annotations JSON** validates the
file and replaces the complete annotation document for the current map. The file must target the
currently loaded map.

Schema:

```json
{
  "schema": "qw-nav-annotations/1",
  "map": "dm3",
  "layers": [
    {
      "name": "Hex bowl review",
      "visible": true,
      "arrows": [
        {
          "from": [480.0, -256.0, 56.0],
          "to": [672.0, -256.0, 56.0],
          "color": [34, 224, 255]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

Layer names are case-insensitively unique, printable, and 1–80 characters. Coordinates must be
finite and an arrow must have non-zero length.

<!-- runbook:nav-editor -->
## Nav editor and patches

The Nav editor authors pure `qw-nav-patch/1` data. It never changes a graph or server by itself.

### Prerequisites

1. Load the exact exported Nano graph that the candidate targets.
2. Click **Frame Nano graph** if the relevant cells are hard to see.
3. Enable **Edit mode**. The editor refuses to arm without an exported graph.

### Add a link

1. Choose `JumpGap`, `SpeedJump`, `Drop`, or `Walk`.
2. For `SpeedJump`, enter a positive finite `v_req`.
3. Click the source cell, then the target cell.
4. The takeoff defaults to the source-cell edge facing the target.
5. Drag the magenta takeoff handle in the XY plane or enter exact X/Y/Z values under
   **Selected takeoff handle**.

Patch additions are visibly distinct from the base graph. The editor records `v_req` only for
`SpeedJump`.

### Remove a link

Click the middle of an existing visible base-graph link. Clicking again toggles the removal off.
Link-kind filters affect which base links can be selected.

A remove records both the current array link index and source/target coordinates. Link IDs are not
stable across rebuilds; import rebinds a changed ID from the coordinate pair and fails closed if no
matching link exists.

### Import and review

There are three import paths:

- `?graph=<base>&patch=<name>`;
- **Imported patch**, for named `overlays/<name>-patch.json`; or
- **Local patch JSON**, for a downloaded file.

If the patch arrives before its graph, it waits. Once the graph is loaded, map and base must match.
Imported edits remain editable. The status reports add/remove counts and how many remove IDs were
rebound.

### Export

Set **Export name**, then click **Download patch JSON**. The browser downloads
`<name>-patch.json`. **Clear** discards only the current browser working draft.

Schema outline:

```json
{
  "schema": "qw-nav-patch/1",
  "map": "dm3",
  "base": "dm3-graph",
  "adds": [
    {
      "kind": "SpeedJump",
      "from": [480.0, -256.0, 56.0],
      "takeoff": [496.0, -256.0, 56.0],
      "to": [672.0, -256.0, 56.0],
      "v_req": 320.0
    }
  ],
  "removes": [
    {
      "link": 11897,
      "from": [240.0, -32.0, 56.0],
      "to": [240.0, -32.0, -200.0]
    }
  ]
}
```

### Critical DEV-poll rule

`overlays/dm3-patch.json` is a live, driver-polled DEV surface. Never copy or export a candidate to
that path silently. Author to a non-polled draft filename, obtain review, and make the plan row and
live export one atomic delivery:

```bash
python3 ops/planlog.py export \
  --seat navmesh-worker \
  --file docs/plans/2026-07-19-spawn7-orchestration.md \
  --patch /path/to/reviewed-draft-patch.json \
  --dest qw-nav-viewer/overlays/dm3-patch.json \
  --text "Reviewed patch identity, graph facit, and expected effect"
```

Do not use this command before the applicable review order allows the live export.

<!-- runbook:ghost-route -->
## Ghost bot and route goal

The **Ghost bot** and **Route goal** panels exercise the viewer's local diagnostic navigation:

1. Enter requested X/Y/Z values for the ghost and goal.
2. Click **Place ghost & plan route**.
3. Both endpoints snap to the nearest standable Hull-1 cell.
4. `nav-core` builds an 8-unit near field around the ghost and runs its four-band local A*.

Keyboard alternatives are `G` for the ghost and `P` for the goal at the camera position.

This route does not query Nano's live planner, even when an exported Nano graph is displayed. Use
it for local topology and clearance diagnostics, not as proof that the game bot selects or executes
the same route.

<!-- runbook:visual-legend -->
## Visual legend and status

The left legend explains:

- speed-band route colors: slow/large turn, run, fast, and bhop-preserved;
- near-field cells: walkable, wall/repulsion, and drop; and
- the bright chord-clear glide that may replace a coarse local zigzag.

The bottom status bar reports the loaded map, local cell/link counts, path segments and planned
time, glide length, and near-field counts. The graph, overlay, patch, and annotation panels each
have their own success/error status line. Read those lines before treating a visual as valid.

<!-- runbook:fly-camera -->
## Fly-camera help panel

The final left-column panel repeats the essential fly controls so they stay visible in the app:
`WASD`, `Space`/`C`, `Shift`, right-mouse look, `G`, and `P`. The complete control table is in
[Maps and camera](#maps-and-camera).

## URL recipes

Parameters may be combined:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8088/?overlay=dm3-spawns
http://127.0.0.1:8088/?graph=dm3
http://127.0.0.1:8088/?graph=dm3&patch=dm3
http://127.0.0.1:8088/?overlay=dm3-spawns&graph=dm3&patch=dm3
http://127.0.0.1:8088/?graph=dm3p8&overlay=dm3p8-bowl-crossings&kinds=SpeedJump
```

Supported query parameters are `overlay`, `graph`, `patch`, and `kinds`. `kinds` is a
comma-separated, case-insensitive list of exported link kinds to show, for example
`kinds=SpeedJump,JumpGap`. Map choice is currently a UI selection; an overlay or graph may switch
the map through its `map` field.

## Troubleshooting

### Page cannot be reached

```bash
systemctl --user status qw-nav-viewer.service --no-pager
ss -ltnp | rg ':8088|:18089'
curl -fsS -I http://127.0.0.1:8088/
journalctl --user -u qw-nav-viewer.service -n 100 --no-pager
```

Restart the service only after reading the failure. Do not start a second Trunk process on `8088`.

### UI loads but the 3D view is black

- Wait for the status bar to leave `Initializing WebGPU…`.
- Perform a hard reload (`Ctrl+F5`).
- Check the browser console for WebGPU adapter/device errors.
- Use current Chrome or Edge with WebGPU enabled; software/headless Chromium may expose a canvas
  while rendering it black.
- Confirm `data-frame` and `data-gpu` are advancing on the `#status` element during automated QA.

### Overlay, graph, or patch returns 404

- Verify the exact source filename under `overlays/`.
- Remember the suffix rules: `<name>.json`, `<name>-graph.json`, and `<name>-patch.json`.
- Probe the no-cache source directly:

```bash
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:18089/<exact-file>.json
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8088/overlays/<exact-file>.json
```

### An overlay looks stale

The source server sends `no-store`. Compare disk bytes with both URLs above and inspect the service
journal. Do not copy JSON into `dist/`; the live proxy intentionally avoids that stale-copy path.

### Draw mode does nothing

- Make sure **Draw mode** is checked and **Nav editor → Edit mode** is off.
- Start and release on a visible navmesh surface, not arbitrary BSP geometry.
- Load or show the graph surface you intend to annotate.
- A click without a meaningful drag is rejected as a zero-length arrow.

### Annotations disappeared on another device

Browser autosave is intentionally device/profile-local. Download the annotation JSON on the source
device and import it on the destination device.

### Nav editor will not arm

Load an exported graph first. The local diagnostic carve cannot be patched.

### Patch import fails

Read the panel status. Common causes are a wrong map, wrong `base`, unsupported schema/kind,
non-finite coordinates, invalid `SpeedJump.v_req`, or a remove coordinate pair absent from the
current graph rebuild.

## Build and release validation

Run from `qw-nav-viewer/`:

```bash
cargo fmt --all -- --check
cargo test --workspace
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings
NO_COLOR=true trunk build
python3 scripts/verify_p8_focus.py
```

`cargo test --workspace` includes the runbook panel-coverage guard, bundled-map parsing, DM3 local
graph/near-field checks, contract parser tests, editor rebinding, annotation round trips, camera
projection, free surface picking, and eraser hit-testing.

For a user-visible change, also open the running app in a real browser and verify the affected flow
end to end. A valid visual check records:

- the URL and selected map/graph/overlay/patch;
- the panel status text;
- a screenshot containing both the relevant controls and rendered result; and
- console errors plus the `#status` frame/GPU diagnostics.

After validation, keep `qw-nav-viewer.service` active and verify:

```bash
curl -fsS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://127.0.0.1:8088/
systemctl --user is-active qw-nav-viewer.service
```

## Source map

| Area | Source of truth |
|---|---|
| UI controls | `index.html`, `style.css` |
| Application/input/state | `nav-viewer/src/main.rs` |
| BSP and overlay rendering | `nav-viewer/src/gpu.rs`, `geom.rs`, `overlay.rs` |
| Local diagnostic navigation | `nav-core/` |
| Overlay contract | `nav-viewer/src/overlay_data.rs` |
| Exported graph contract | `nav-viewer/src/graph_data.rs` |
| Patch contract | `nav-viewer/src/patch_data.rs` |
| Annotation contract | `nav-viewer/src/annotation_data.rs` |
| Live serving | `scripts/serve-dev.sh`, `scripts/live_overlays.py`, `Trunk.toml` |
| p8 focus integrity | `scripts/verify_p8_focus.py` |
| Fixtures and live data | `overlays/` |
| Visual evidence | `validation/`, transient `output/playwright/` |


