1-// SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
2-
3-//! External bot-control channel — the rocket-jump tuning harness.
4-//!
5-//! A cvar-gated (`rtx_control_port`) localhost TCP server an external driver connects to in order to
6-//! *puppet* a bot: teleport it to a rocket-jump link's launch cell, order it to fly that specific
7-//! link, and read back per-attempt telemetry (stance offset, aim error, fire-timing error, landing
8://! miss). The point is a scripted tuning loop — sweep every RJ link a map generates, see which land,
9-//! turn the `rtx_rj_*` knobs, re-run — without hand-flying bots in a live server.
10-//!
11-//! ## Threading
12://! The engine drives this module single-threaded from the frame calls, so every `GameState` mutation
13-//! stays on that thread. The socket work is pushed to background threads that only shuttle raw
14-//! `String` lines through `mpsc` channels — the exact shape as the navmesh build worker
15-//! ([`crate::nav_build`]): a listener thread accepts connections, a per-connection reader thread feeds
16-//! inbound lines to [`ControlState::lines_rx`], and a writer thread drains outbound JSON to the
17://! current client. Commands are parsed and executed, and events emitted, entirely inside
18://! [`frame_begin`]/[`frame_end`] under the frame's `&mut GameState`. No lock is ever held over game
19-//! state; the only shared state between threads is the raw socket and the channels.
20-//!
21-//! ## Protocol
22-//! Inbound: newline-delimited text, `<id> <verb> [args…]`, `id` a caller-chosen integer echoed back.
23-//! Outbound: newline-delimited hand-emitted JSON (the game crate stays dependency-free). A reply is
24-//! `{"id":N,"ok":true,"data":{…}}` / `{"id":N,"ok":false,"error":"…"}`; an unsolicited lifecycle
25://! event is `{"ev":"arrived"|"goto_stall"|"rj_result",…}`. A single outbound channel gives total
26://! ordering; the client demuxes on the presence of `id` vs `ev`.
